<!--
  Deliberately NO validate-jwt, which is where this diverges from
  api-cp-crime-schedulingandlisting-courtschedule's policy of the same name.

  That policy validates a corporate Entra token against login.microsoftonline.com and rejects any
  request without an Authorization header. Both halves would be wrong here:

  1. This sandbox issues its OWN tokens, from POST /oauth2/v2.0/token, signed with a key held in
     its key vault and verified in-process by the EntraTokenValidator copied byte-for-byte from the
     live service. Microsoft has never heard of them, so validate-jwt would reject every one. The
     whole point of the sandbox is that a consumer meets the real validator's behaviour - including
     its 401s and its 403 insufficient_scope - so moving that enforcement to the edge would delete
     the thing being demonstrated.

  2. Every path in try-it-now-public.openapi.yml is unauthenticated by design. GET /scenarios is
     where the demo credentials come from, so it has to be readable before a consumer holds
     anything; the same goes for the token endpoint and JWKS. Returning 401 on a missing header
     would make the sandbox impossible to start using.

  What is left is throughput protection, which matters more here than on an internal API: this is a
  public surface with published credentials. The counter falls back to the caller's IP, since an
  API with subscription_required = false has no subscription key to count against.

  /admin/** is absent from the public contract and is not registered in APIM at all - it is guarded
  by AdminAuthFilter and a real corporate Entra token. See contracts/README.md.
-->
<policies>
    <inbound>
        <rate-limit-by-key calls="30" renewal-period="60" counter-key="@((context.Subscription?.Key ?? context.Request.IpAddress) + &quot;:try-slc&quot;)" />
        <quota-by-key calls="500" renewal-period="86400" counter-key="@((context.Subscription?.Key ?? context.Request.IpAddress) + &quot;:try-slc&quot;)" />
        <base />
    </inbound>
    <backend>
        <limit-concurrency key="@((context.Subscription?.Key ?? context.Request.IpAddress) + &quot;:try-slc&quot;)" max-count="10">
            <forward-request />
        </limit-concurrency>
    </backend>
    <outbound>
        <base />
    </outbound>
    <on-error>
        <base />
    </on-error>
</policies>
